{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7ETZj4yRsDdqFrtW8H5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draft, a few questions. Your answers will change what the email says:\n\n1. **Who gets this email, and will the 14 Maple Street and Route 9 staff hear it first?** For example, will they have meetings with their managers the same morning? It matters whether this is how they find out or whether it confirms what they've already been told.\n2. **What is the deadline for accepting the new role, and who should people contact?** Also, is there anything else in the severance besides 2 weeks of pay per year, such as continued benefits, a cap, or a minimum?\n3. **How do the rotating shifts work?** Will anyone regularly work until 8 p.m.? Is there a shift differential, or any say over which shifts people get? The 8-to-8 schedule is likely to be the first thing the 14 worry about.\n4. **Where is the center, and is training paid at full rate?** If some people will have a longer commute during the 90 days before they can work from home, I'd rather mention it than leave it out.\n5. **Concretely, what makes the job better?** You want the email to say this change improves everyone's job. Staff moving from a 9-to-5 branch to rotating phone shifts may not believe that unless they can see specifics. Is there a pay increase for some people, a career path, new skills or tools, or less pressure once hold times come down? Is there a target for the 9-minute wait? If you don't have specifics, I'd suggest a more modest claim that staff are more likely to believe.\n\nIf you don't know something yet, just tell me and I'll write around it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":260,"cache_read_input_tokens":9574,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":260},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standa